From 34347e5d878ddb55c69260a5baa6b39270b3917b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dominggoes Isakh <drjisakh@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:15:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Jelly: Fix downloads from forms, blobs and
 Content-Disposition names

- Parse Content-Disposition as RFC 6266 does: filename* (RFC 5987) wins
  over filename, any disposition type, quoted or unquoted names with
  other parameters after them, and percent-encoded names. Before, only
  "attachment; filename=..." at the end of the header was understood and
  most downloads were named after the URL ("cd1.pdf", "plain.zip;
  size=22").
- Use the name from <a download="..."> when the server gives none, and
  give DownloadManager the server's MIME type instead of one guessed from
  the URL.
- Downloads that answer a form POST were fetched again with GET by
  DownloadManager and failed. A script injected into each page records
  POST form submissions (submit events and form.submit()); when the
  download arrives, Jelly sends the same POST itself, with the cookies
  only for a same-origin form, and saves the response in Downloads.
  Forms with file inputs still fall back to GET.
- blob: and data: URLs were dropped ("Cannot download non http or https
  scheme"). data: URLs are decoded, and blob: URLs are read through the
  page that made them; the script fetches a blob as soon as its link is
  clicked, because pages usually revoke the URL right after.
- The JavaScript interface only accepts calls with a secret that the
  injected script has, and needs keep rules so R8 doesn't strip it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I8328aca8069bfb06405b079ae5c9c92e47ad885a
